{red}-1$ | $4$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1} = 4 -\\dfrac{5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-\\frac{5}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{0}^{1}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[4-0]-5[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =4-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064097 #edb/ref 1069 "e-486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064097 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74761123-d539-5d78-8dcd-4ea1db04bed0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064097 #edb/ref 1074 "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064098 #edb/ref 1069 "e-487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064098 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf016fa7-c3a2-5f9b-84a6-7b005d0d7286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064098 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1759218633271